      "raw_text": "To find out how much money John makes in a week, we need to break down the problem into steps: calculating the total number of apartments, determining the total number of collections per week, and finally calculating the total earnings.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the number of apartments in the first two complexes.**\nThe problem states that the first two complexes have 200 apartments each.\n$$200 \\text{ apartments} \\times 2 = 400 \\text{ apartments}$$\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the number of apartments in the third complex.**\nThe third complex is 60% bigger than the other two combined.\nFirst, find 60% of the combined total of the first two complexes (400 apartments):\n$$400 \\times 0.60 = 240 \\text{ apartments}$$\nNow, add this increase to the original combined total to get the size of the third complex:\n$$400 + 240 = 640 \\text{ apartments}$$\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the total number of apartments across all three complexes.**\nAdd the apartments from the first two complexes and the third complex:\n$$400 + 640 = 1040 \\text{ apartments}$$\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the total number of collections John makes in a week.**\nJohn collects garbage 3 times a week from *each* place. Since there are 3 different apartment complexes:\n$$3 \\text{ collections/week} \\times 3 \\text{ complexes} = 9 \\text{ collections per week}$$\n\n**Step 5: Calculate the total number of individual apartment collections.**\nFor each of the 9 weekly collection trips, he visits every apartment in the complexes.\n$$9 \\text{ trips} \\times 1040 \\text{ apartments} = 9360 \\text{ individual collections}$$\n\n**Step 6: Calculate the total earnings.**\nJohn gets paid $0.40 per collection.\n$$9360 \\text{ collections} \\times \\$0.40 = \\$3744.00$$\n\nAlternatively, you can calculate the total collections per complex first:\n- Total apartments = 1040\n- Collections per week per complex = 3\n- Total collections per week = $1040 \\",
      "answer_text": "To find out how much money John makes in a week, we need to break down the problem into steps: calculating the total number of apartments, determining the total number of collections per week, and finally calculating the total earnings.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the number of apartments in the first two complexes.**\nThe problem states that the first two complexes have 200 apartments each.\n$$200 \\text{ apartments} \\times 2 = 400 \\text{ apartments}$$\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the number of apartments in the third complex.**\nThe third complex is 60% bigger than the other two combined.\nFirst, find 60% of the combined total of the first two complexes (400 apartments):\n$$400 \\times 0.60 = 240 \\text{ apartments}$$\nNow, add this increase to the original combined total to get the size of the third complex:\n$$400 + 240 = 640 \\text{ apartments}$$\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the total number of apartments across all three complexes.**\nAdd the apartments from the first two complexes and the third complex:\n$$400 + 640 = 1040 \\text{ apartments}$$\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the total number of collections John makes in a week.**\nJohn collects garbage 3 times a week from *each* place. Since there are 3 different apartment complexes:\n$$3 \\text{ collections/week} \\times 3 \\text{ complexes} = 9 \\text{ collections per week}$$\n\n**Step 5: Calculate the total number of individual apartment collections.**\nFor each of the 9 weekly collection trips, he visits every apartment in the complexes.\n$$9 \\text{ trips} \\times 1040 \\text{ apartments} = 9360 \\text{ individual collections}$$\n\n**Step 6: Calculate the total earnings.**\nJohn gets paid $0.40 per collection.\n$$9360 \\text{ collections} \\times \\$0.40 = \\$3744.00$$\n\nAlternatively, you can calculate the total collections per complex first:\n- Total apartments = 1040\n- Collections per week per complex = 3\n- Total collections per week = $1040 \\",
